Subject: Cantor EVO-X2 Needle 2 SOP Attention Runtime Justification

@ [justification_uuid] 60f16470-fdba-4feb-b4ca-236f81c40578
@ [canonical] C:\Project\Cantor\specifications\Cantor_EVO_X2_Needle2_SOP_Attention_Runtime.sop

& [Need] is a live learned procedure-selection seam
  + Cantor already proves deterministic trust, query, procedure, and checkpoint mechanics but does not have a resident learned selector
  + the existing llama.cpp probe proves tool emission and later-pass import but its tool result is a hard-coded fixture
  + Needle 2 is purpose-built for constrained calls and can remain small enough to stay resident beside a larger provider

& [Choice] is a separated sidecar
  + retain Cantor Rust as the authoritative verifier and executor
  + let Needle 2 propose one schema-constrained operation with advisory confidence
  + let unmodified llama.cpp articulate only after the verified AttentionFrame exists
  + use content addressing for exact procedure identity while keeping signatures and authorization distinct

& [Rejected] avoids premature collapse
  + loading all SOP into Needle exceeds its bounded context and confuses retrieval with authority
  + treating model weights or embeddings as the contract makes procedures uninspectable and unauditable
  + modifying llama.cpp is unnecessary because the external checkpoint seam already works
  + merging the Narrative personality into this runtime would mix user adaptation with trust-kernel behavior

& [ImplementationLanguage] follows dependency shape
  + Python is selected for the experimental controller because the official Needle 2 package exposes its supported engine and the existing EVO-X2 Python 3.12 toolchain is installed
  + Cantor remains Rust and is invoked through its existing strict process protocol
  + a Rust or embedded C adapter is deferred until the protocol and workload are proven

& [ReasoningDepth] requires current deep integration judgment
  + boundary formation and live dependency reconciliation require deeper reasoning
  + after exact fixtures and contracts exist, additional test cases and documentation become Spark-suitable bounded work
